00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.720 align:start position:0%
 
thank you very much for being with us um  so what do you make of Sam Juma entering  the race well it's obviously creating  quite a crowded field he certainly took  us by surprise this morning but you know  it's part of what needs to be a big  conversation I get in the guess and the  Conservative Party about the type of  leadership that they wanted the reason  that I'm standing is because I think we  need a different type of leadership at  this time  that's about more than shouting at the  other side and bossing everybody about  we have to carefully put together the  governing party into a majority that  will deliver a brexit deal which is what  I did back in January when the what  became known as the multi house  compromise emerged and produced the  majority in the House of Commons and so  looking for a different way of leading  the party that as I say carefully puts  together the different wings of the  party in an agreement that can then be  presented to the EU as a way for what I  think is critical for us now is that is  there any evidence though there anyone  else agrees with you that the ones have  a different style of leadership in the  Conservative Party with respect no one  is talking about you as the next prime  minister well I've got a number of  supporters who have declared already and  hopefully over the next few weeks I will  persuade people that's the case I mean  one of the things we have to get away  from I think is a kind of bifurcated  debate where you know that the  competition itself becomes a problem  after the event so that to put the thing  back together again and one of the  problems that we've had during the whole  brexit debate has been this sort of  binary move into silos if we're going to  find some kind of resolution before the  31st of October we have to bring  everybody out of those silos put them in  a room together find some way to  navigate towards an agreement that  everybody can focus on to get us over  the line for brexit now as I say we  managed to do that back in January  people as far apart as Nicky Morgan and  Jacob Riis morg and Steve Baker came  into a room together we sat down  together over a big bowl of chocolate to  get a deal hammered out and I think it's  as I say it takes a different kind of  leadership to make that happen the  greatest achievement of that of the  Malthouse compromise again with respect  it's perhaps more than the compromise  element rather than the Malthouse part  of it as you say political opponents did  put aside  their differences it's just unfortunate  that they did so for a plan described by  the European Union variously as bonkers  and just nonsense yeah so when the  compromise came out it was at a  particular time I think where the EU was  very fixated on getting that withdrawal  agreement through as was the government  and so the contemplation of anything  different was naturally going to be  dismissed right because they wanted what  they wanted now the dynamic has changed  since and what we know now is that the  withdrawal agreement is currently  drafted will not pass the House of  Commons right there we know that the  backstop within it makes that assert it  will not go through and so we have a  duty to find a different kind of  agreement and what I would do as Prime  Minister you know within a shorter space  as possible is present a new type of  agreement and a new agreement that we  know would pass the House of Commons and  say to the EU this is the deal that we  want we're not choosing no deal but this  is the deal that we want and we know we  can get a majority and frankly you have  a duty as we have a duty to respect your  structures and democracy you have a duty  to respect ours and that means that  coming together as a group of great  democracies we have to find a way  through critical to that will be of  course dealing with the situation in  Ireland I was in Northern Ireland on  Friday  talking to groups they're constructively  about how we might try and find a way  through for their legitimate concerns  the complexities of that particular  situation and so a constructive  leadership that puts together a  governing majority I think will be  critical at this time but just to  examine your strategizing when the last  plan that you came up with involved  technology that doesn't exist yet and  I'm still waiting for a hoverboard  yeah that's not correct the range you're  talking about the European Union about  coming up with something fresh to do so  you need to compromise you will need to  give ground to the European Union where  does that going to happen  so first of all that's a common  misconception this technological thing I  mean I know it was it took off in the  press but in truth what the groups  around alternative arrangements have  pointed out repeatedly is that relies on  existing systems and technology checks  away from the border trusted trader  there's lots of complexity in there but  nevertheless existing stuff and there's  no there's not really in the world where  there aren't actually checks on the  border that's critically of course in  the second set of negotiations that took  place both the EU and the UK government  committed to  this idea of alternative arrangements  and there is significant work going on  in fact mr. Katainen who's the vice  president of the Commission gave a press  conference back in April where he said  they are specifically working on a new  system for checks at the border in  northern Iran to avoid a visible border  so there is work going on and there is a  just a question of when that happens not  so if yes so whereas they go where's the  compromise  well the compromise is that we as I say  we will put forward a an agreement that  we know will garner support in the House  of Commons  now the will there's possible to be some  negotiation around that within that we  want to have will obviously have to have  some kind of option of a transition  agreement but where we want to find  agreement with the EU is because both  sides want to try and avoid no deal and  we have to reverse the dynamic of the  negotiation so we put it back on to the  EU that if they won't take a deal which  is eminently reasonable which has  consent through democratic processes in  the UK then they are effectively  pointing away to No Deal for ours and  for them and that's something that's  profoundly to be avoided so you think  that the suggestion from Michel gulfs  camp this morning that there could be a  delay to brexit of as much as a year  perhaps even longer than that to get a  deal done you think that's a good idea  no I don't I've haven't voted to support  any kind of extension to the process now  I think you could see a short technical  extension if you needed it if a  principal had been agreed you know worth  the wait for an agreement that was in  place but I think we have to be honest  with ourselves that wherever we if we  get to the 31st of October  without an agreement in place then there  are no good choices available to a prime  minister at that point you know and we  will be on a hair-trigger for a general  election which could be devastating for  the country and catapult it into a sort  of chaotic mix of who knows what so we  need to get a deal through and the only  way I think we can do that is by  presenting something which fundamentally  has the consent of the British  Parliament first and that means as I say  a new kind of leadership that puts that  deal together  I mean you are Housing Minister I mean  there is something of a crisis in  housing and the moment steps have been  taken me don't you have better things to  do well I certainly am spending a lot of  my time on the housing that sure for  sure and we've did 222 thousand new  houses last year the numbers for next  year were looking good and housing forms  a critical part of my kind of  three-strand program once we're through  brexit of job house school a new social  contract the  these are the building blocks of a  brilliant life you know that we want  fantastic well-funded schools  secure homes for everybody and a stable  job that people can rely on that you  give those three things to most people  and they will fly right so it's it's  central and experience that I've had in  housing over the last few months as  Housing Minister is is critical to  making sure that we hit that big number  of 300,000 homes by the mid-2020s

